<https://loze-ontology.org/> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Loze Foundation" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An upper-level ontology largely inspired by the Four-Category Ontology outlined by the philosopher Jonathan Lowe, supplemented with the concept of a schematic particular explored by Eddy Zemach" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Actor> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Actor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Actor" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Actor> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:suggested-social-extension_cbe5a1ad522aaffaca47d3ebe78e2851770e4e4ec5c5e7b1340ec3d90610a8cd85 <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Actor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An object that is either a person or an organisation" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Actor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "It roughly corresponds to the legal concept of a legal person" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Actor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "This class is meant to encompass all the objects that can deliberate, make decisions, and bear responsibility" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Aggregate" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A thing that is individuated by some stable material composition or spatial contiguity" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A chunk of wood, a waterbody, the liquid contained in a tank, a heap of sand, the global petrol supply" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An aggregate can also be scattered across space - such as the total water reserve of England or the amount of methane used for heating my house over the last year - in which case its individuation is based on a combination of material composition and practical interests" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Aggregate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Objects are also typically material, but aggregates differ from objects because they are not unified by an internal organisation, function or form" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Animal" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:suggested-social-extension_cbcefc463dac54600c9f991106fcda4fca649e7d4adecf9d99a69874e305bb5d7c <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A living being that (i) is eukaryote - namely, has cells with a membrane-bound nucleus and organelles - and (ii) is heterotrophic - that is, derives its nourishment from the consumption of other living beings" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Animals typically have a more flexible body than plants and fungi and are able to move" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Within the taxonomy of living beings - so-called Tree of Life - animals are the members of the kingdom of Animalia" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Artefact" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An object that is designed and made by humans for serving certain purposes or functions" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A vehicle, a hammer, an appliance, a duct, a glass, a house, a cloth, an electronic device, a fork, a software, an artwork" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Artefact can be either schematic or concrete: a software - in abstraction from its installed copies - is an artefact; a software's installed copies are also artefacts; a symphony - as a work of music, in abstraction from its performances - is also an artefact" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Composition" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An eventuality that consists in one entity being made up or incorporating some other entity" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In Loze Foundation, this class only has two subclasses, namely loze:Parthood and loze:Constitution; however, other subclasses are defined in Loze's extensions, and further subclasses should be freely defined whenever needed" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Concrete Particular" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A particular that is bounded to one spatial location at a time" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "Your arm, one Toyota Corolla unit, the computer in front of you, the portion of water in a given glass, a piece of gold, one performance of Beethoven's Symphony No 9, the stabbing of Caesar from Brutus, a heap of sand, your bedroom, the last hour" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Differently from Schematic particulars, a concrete particular only occupies one place or location at a time: my copy of War and Peace occupies one slot in my bookcase right now; I can only occupy one place at a time; a unit of Toyota Corolla is in one place at a time; a particular performance of Beethoven's Symphony No 9 takes place in one and only one venue" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/ConcreteParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The distinction between concrete particulars and schematic particulars cuts across all the other divisions of the class :Particular: there are concrete objects and schematic objects, concrete eventualities and schematic eventualities, concrete aggregates and schematic aggregates, and so on" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Constitution" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A composition that consists in one particular being entirely made up by repeated occurrences of a given schematic particular" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A polymerous sequence being made up by repetitions of a given monomer; an aggregate of tablets being made up by one tablet (construed as a schematic object) repeated in 10 occurrences" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In a constitution, the core participants are the composite (loze:isCompositeIn) and the schematic particular that is repeated in it (loze:isComponentIn)" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In formal mereology, one particular being 'entirely made up' by repeated occurrences, means that it is identical to the mereological sum of those occurrences: as an approximation, this means that its only parts are those occurrences and the parts of those occurrences; more rigorous definitions can be found in the philosophical literature on formal mereology" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Constitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In practice, the function of this class of eventualities is to describe the overall composition of a particular without giving an enumeration of its individual parts" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Containment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Containment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Containment" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Containment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Containment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An eventuality in which a particular is positioned within and physically constrained by another object" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Containment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A box containing eggs, a glass containing a portion of water, a dwelling containing pieces of furniture" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Containment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In a containment, the core participants are the container (loze:isHostIn) and the content (loze:isContentIn)" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Entity" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:root_cb181aff8bb272e5ae2ce5ec6912a427e444ac9f205ee7bc5ad80854b19f00dc76b <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Anything that exists, is real, or is in the world" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "It corresponds to owl:Thing" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Eventuality" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A particular entity that is a way of being or a modification of one or more other entities - qualifying them or making them be or go in a certain way - and is individuated by those entities" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "The redness of this apple, the wisdom of Socrates, the marriage between Napoleon and Josephine, the stabbing of Caesar from Brutus, my walk from my house to a grocery shop, the Earth being positioned between Venus and Mars, an engine being fuelled with an amount of gasoline" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An eventuality is not general, but is linked to its participants - that is, the entities that bear it or are involved in it; the wisdom of Socrates is numerically distinct from the wisdom of Diogenes, and they existed at different times; the redness of this apple is distinct from the redness of that other apple, and so on" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "This is intended as a broad, comprehensive category that encompasses the entities that in other ontologies and category systems are variously called 'qualities', 'attributes', 'relations, 'events', 'processes', 'facts', 'specifically dependent continuants' etc..." <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Group" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An object that consists of persons that are associated to each other by some socially significant relations or recurrent interactions" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A household, a family, a couple, any organisation" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Groups don't include collections of persons who merely happen to have a common feature - such as the men, the persons with blonde hair, or the graduates - unless those persons also happen to be organised together or to interact with each other on a regular basis; if needed, such collections are best modelled as classes" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Information Artefact" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An artefact that consists of a structured system of symbols' occurrences meant to convey information" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A book, an article, a note, a document, a digital file, a computer programme or software" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An information artefact can be either a schematic object or a concrete object: War and Peace is schematic, whereas its copies are concrete; a PDF file is schematic, whereas its copies are concrete; a software is schematic, whereas its installed copies are concrete" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An information artefact that is concrete is always going to be the concretization of some schematic information artefact" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The information carried by an information artefact does not need to be intelligible for humans: for example, certain files or codes carry information that is only meant to be executed by a machine" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Living Being" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An object that displays properties such as responsiveness, growth, metabolism, energy transformation, and reproduction" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The precise definition of life and living being is controversial in philosophy of science and theoretical biology; still, the marks described in our definition are normally sufficient for assessing whether an object is a living being or not; that being said, there are a few borderline cases, such as viruses and so-called super-organisms (e.g. coral colonies and bacterial biofilms)" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Membership> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Membership> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Membership" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Membership> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Membership> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A composition that consists in an actor joining the goals and/or th activities of a group" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Membership> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A person being a member of a household, a person being a member of a club or charity, a person being an employee of a company, a company being a member of a trade association, France being a member of the European Union" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Membership> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In a membership, the main participants are a member (loze:isMemberIn) and a composite (loze:isCompositeIn): the former is the actor that is the member, and the latter is the group of which they are a member" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Non-Human Animal" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An animal that is not a specimen of the species Homo sapiens" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/NonHumanAnimal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In ordinary talk as well as legal talk, non-human animals are usually called just 'animals'" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Object" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A thing that is individuated by a certain internal organisation, function, or form" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A human, a horse, a molecule, a table, a computer, a planet, a paracetamol tablet" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Object> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An object persists over time as long as it preserves the internal organisation, function, or form that individuates it; as long as that is preserved, the parts of the objects and their types can change without causing the object to go out of existence" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Organisation" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A group whose members are acting together in the pursuit of one or more common goals, based on an agreed upon system of commitments and rules" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A company, a charity or foundation, a political party, a club, a state or government, an international organisation, a criminal gang, a terrorist organisation" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "A group does not need to be legally registered or recognised to qualify as an organisation; in fact, there are organisations that are illegal by definition, such as criminal gangs and terrorist organisations" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An organisation can also have other organisations as parts: for example, a company can have other companies - its subsidiaries - as parts, and an international organisation can have governments as parts" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Organisation Subdivision" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Group> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://loze-ontology.org/Organisation> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A group that (i) is part of an organisation, (ii) it serves a specific function in the organisation in question, and (iii) it not itself an organisation" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A government's parliament, a company's committee, a team" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/OrganisationSubdivision> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The difference between an organisation subdivision and an organisation that is part of another organisation is that the official goals of a subdivision are entirely subordinated to those of the organisation of which it is part; by contrast, an organisation can maintain its own goals despite being part of a larger organisation" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Parthood> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Parthood> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Parthood" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Parthood> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Composition> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Parthood> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A composition that consists in one particular being part of another particular" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Parthood> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "An arm being part of a person, a wall being part of a house, a compressor being part of a refrigerator, a wood board being part of a table, a nucleus being part of an atom, Mars being part of the solar system, the lithosphere being part of the Earth, a movement being part of symphony, hydrogen atom being part of water molecule" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Parthood> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The core participants in a parthood are the part (loze:isComponentIn) and the composite (loze:isCompositeIn)" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Particular" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Entity> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:root_cb6d5e66db88ab040ed2d20890f1f9d9299f444b7ccda9509b20f0fe658ef81c7a <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:root_cbb01a60d583694f546220d76ef762743e4eca93a451a4e0e46ea3ef0cfcfe59a3 <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An entity that is not a class or type of entities" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Particular entities are to be contrasted with classes; the concept of a particular mostly corresponds to the Semantic Web concept of an individual or instance" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Period Of Time" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A thing that is individuated by its coordinates along the temporal axis" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "The year 1943, the 6th of May of 2005, any second or minute, the period that goes from the 3rd of May of 1467 to the 22nd of July of 1615" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Two period of time can have exactly the same duration and yet be distinct period of time, because they have distinct beginnings and ends; for example, this hour and the next hour are two distinct periods, despite having the exact same duration of 60 minutes" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Person> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Person> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Person" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Person> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Person> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An animal that is a specimen of the species Homo sapiens" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Person> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "It does not encompass those entities that in the legal jargon are called 'legal persons' or 'non-physical persons' " <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Person> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Specimens of other species of the genus Homo (such as Homo erectus or Homo Neanderthalensis) would be problematic to categorise, because they are not specimens of Homo sapiens yet they qualify as humans in a broader sense; in practice, this issue is irrelevant, since Homo sapiens is the only extant species of the genus Homo" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Place" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A thing that is individuated by its position relative to other things - such as being enclosed by an object, being to the left side of an object, or being immediately North of another place" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A room, the interior of a refrigerator, a tunnel, the left ventricle of your heart" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The matter that is hosted in a place makes no difference for the individuation of that place: a room remains in existence irrespective of whether it hosts an absolute vacuum, a few pieces of furniture and regular air, or a giant load of cotton wool" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "There is an obvious sense in which even objects can host other particulars: for example, a house can host people and artefacts; likewise, a tank can host amounts of liquid; however, places host things by being occupied by them, whereas objects host things by having them within themselves - that is, by having them occupying the place that they enclose" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Place Occupation" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An eventuality that consists in one particular entity occupying a place partly or entirely" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A carrot occupying the interior of a refrigerator, a sofa occupying a living room, an amount of liquid occupying the interior of a tank" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In a place occupation, the host is always a place, rather than the object enclosing it - say, the interior of a refrigerator, rather than the refrigerator itself" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/PlaceOccupation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The core participants in a place occupation are the place (loze:isHostIn) and the particular entity that occupies it (loze:isContentIn)" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Plant" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/LivingBeing> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://loze-ontology.org/Animal> <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A living being that (i) is eukaryote - namely, has cells with a membrane-bound nucleus and organelles - and (ii) derives its nourishment from photosynthesis" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Plants typically have a firmer cellular structure than animals and are not able to move" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Plant> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Within the taxonomy of living beings - so-called Tree of Life - plants are the members of the kingdom of Plantae" <https://loze-ontology.org/suggested-social-extension> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Schematic Particular" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A particular that is not bounded to one spatial location at a time and that can be concretized by concrete particulars" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A document (as opposed to its particular copies), a work of music (as opposed to its performances), an alphabetic character (as opposed to its inscriptions), a software (as opposed to its installed copies), a substance or material like water or gold (as opposed to their circumscribed portions), a car model (as opposed to its units)" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "An schematic particular has the force of a determinant with regard to its concretizations: the structure of Beethoven's Symphony No 9 dictates the structure of its performances; the specifications of the Toyota Corolla are the benchmark for the features of its units; the chemical properties of water determine the behaviour of concrete portions of water" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Differently from classes, schematic particulars share their main features with their instances; for example, a book has chapters just like its copies, a piece of music has the same harmonic structure as its performances, an alphabetic character has the same shape as its inscriptions, a car model has similar features as its units" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Similarly to classes, schematic particulars are not bounded to one particular location at a time: as a rule, a schematic particular is wholly present wherever one of its concretizations is present; War and Peace is present wherever one of its copies is present; water is present wherever one or more H2O molecules are present; the Toyota Corolla is present wherever one of its units is present" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The division between concrete particulars and schematic particulars cuts across the further sub-divisions of particulars: there are concrete objects and schematic objects, concrete eventualities and schematic eventualities, concrete aggregates and schematic aggregates, and so on" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Substance" <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/SchematicParticular> <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:materials-and-composition_cb68067f2b743e19efb21ee5d853c3161b0666d622a2f0455c63f429b69f182231 <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A schematic particular that (i) is either an object or an aggregate and (ii) is highly dissectable" <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "Copper, gold, water, wood, kerosene, steel, wheat flour, sugar, liquid acrylic paint" <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Dissectability consists in the concretizations of a schematic particular being iteratively divisible into parts of the same kind: a mass of water can be divided into other masses of water; a chunk of wood can be further divided into other chunks of wood; a piece of gold can be divided into smaller pieces of gold; and so on. Despite the appearence of infinite divisibility, we know from chemistry that this dissectability is going to stop at either the molecular or the atomic level: for example, a water molecule has no smaller parts that are themselves water, and an atom of gold has no smaller parts that are themselves gold" <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Substance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Here the word 'substance' is used in the chemical sense, not in the metaphysical sense - which originated in ancient Greek philosophy and is still used in contemporary analytic metaphysics" <https://loze-ontology.org/materials-and-composition> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Text" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An information artefact that consists of sentences in some human-readable language and is meant to convey meaning to humans" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "A book, an article, a physical document, a text or document file, a book chapter, a dish recipe" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "According to the definition, not only plain text files, but also document files are instances of loze:Text" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Text> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "In a software system, a machine code might not be a text, because it's not always meant to convey meaning to humans; on the other hand, the source code is a text, since it's human-readable" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Thing" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://loze-ontology.org/Particular> <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:root_cb51b60a82aba4c62426128768cba68d8d6e8446a2b7c55308d832b1c68127c811 <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A particular entity that is not a way of being or a modification of other entities" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/Thing> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Despite the similar local name, this class is much narrower than owl:Thing, which encompasses all entities" <https://loze-ontology.org/> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "after Time" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and a period of time t if and only if e occurs entirely after t" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "WWII occurred after 1900, the birth of Napoleon occurred after 1700" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/afterTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The relation obtains even if the eventuality does not occur immediately after the period of time: for example, WWII occurred after 1900, even though it only started in 1939" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Artefacts, Information, and Instructions" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://loze-ontology.org/> <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An extension of loze-foundation.ttl pertaining to artefacts, information, and instructions" <https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "at Time" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and a period of time t if and only if e is the case or is occurring at t" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "WWII was happening at 1943, the French Revolution was happening at 1790, the Italian Renaissance was happening at 1513" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/atTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "This relation doesn't necessarily relate an eventuality to the entire period of time when it occurs; for example, WWII started in 1939 and ended in 1945, but it was still in course in 1943, so it entertains the relation loze:atTime with 1943 too" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "before Time" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and a period of time t if and only if e occurs entirely before t" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "WWII occurred before 1960, the birth of Napoleon occurred before 1800" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/beforeTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "The relation obtains even if the eventuality does not occur right before the period of time: for example, WWII occurred before 1970, even though over two decades passed between the end of WWII and 1970" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/fromTime> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/fromTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/fromTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "from Time" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/fromTime> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://loze-ontology.org/PeriodOfTime> <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/fromTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and a period of time t if and only if e starts occurring at t" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
<https://loze-ontology.org/fromTime> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example> "WWII occurred from 1939, the Thirty Years' War occurred from 1618, Napoleon's life occurred from 1769" <https://loze-ontology.org/temporal-relations> .
